L2: # DESIGN.md Format
L3: 
L4: DESIGN.md is a self-contained, plain-text representation of a design system. It defines the visual identity of a brand and product, thereby ensuring that these stylistic choices can be followed across design sessions and between different AI agents and tools.  As a human-readable, open-format document, it serves as a living source of truth that both humans and AI can understand and refine.
L5: A DESIGN.md file contains two parts: human-readable markdown prose and machine-readable YAML design tokens. The markdown body sections provide human-readable design rationale and guidance. Prose may use descriptive color names (e.g., "Midnight Forest Green") that correspond to systematic token names (e.g., `primary`). The tokens are the normative values; the prose provides context for how to apply them.
L6: # Design Tokens
L7: 
L8: DESIGN.md may embed design tokens in a structured format. The system that we use to describe design tokens is inspired by the
L9: [Design Token JSON spec](https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/format/#abstract). Specifically, we adopt the concept of typed token groups (colors, typography, spacing) and the `{path.to.token}` reference syntax for cross-referencing values.
L10: 
L11: These tokens are easily converted from or to `tokens.json`, Figma variables, and Tailwind theme configs.
L12: Design tokens may be embedded into `DESIGN.md` in two ways:
L13: 
L14: 1. **YAML Front Matter:** Placed at the beginning of the file. The front matter block must begin with a line containing exactly `---` and end with a line containing exactly `---`.
L15: 2. **Interleaved YAML Code Blocks:** Distributed throughout the document as fenced ` ```yaml ` code blocks placed within the relevant markdown section.
L16: Both approaches may be used together in the same document, for example, placing document metadata like `name` and `description` in the front matter, while placing section tokens like `colors` and `typography` in fenced code blocks inside their respective sections. When multiple YAML blocks are present, all blocks are parsed and merged into a single design token tree.
L17: Each top-level schema key may be defined at most once across the entire document; defining the same top-level key in multiple blocks is an error.
L18: Front Matter Example:
L19: 
L20: ```yaml
L21: ---
L22: version: alpha
L23: name: Daylight Prestige
L24: colors:
L25:   primary: "#1A1C1E"
L26:   secondary: "#6C7278"
L27:   tertiary: "#B8422E"
L28: typography:
L29:   h1:
L30:     fontFamily: Public Sans
L31:     fontSize: 48px
L32:     fontWeight: 600
L33:     lineHeight: 1.1
L34:     letterSpacing: -0.02em
L35: ---
L36: ```
L37: 
L38: Interleaved YAML Code Block Example:
L39: 
L40: ````markdown
L41: ---
L42: version: alpha
L43: name: Daylight Prestige
L44: description: Editorial design system with high-contrast neutrals.
L45: ---
L46: ## Colors
L47: 
L48: The palette is rooted in high-contrast neutrals and a single accent color.
L49: 
L50: ```yaml
L51: colors:
L52:   primary: "#1A1C1E"
L53:   secondary: "#6C7278"
L54:   tertiary: "#B8422E"
L55: ```
L56: 
L57: ## Typography
L58: 
L59: The typography strategy leverages Public Sans for headlines and narrative.
L60: 
L61: ```yaml
L62: typography:
L63:   h1:
L64:     fontFamily: Public Sans
L65:     fontSize: 48px
L66:     fontWeight: 600
L67:     lineHeight: 1.1
L68:     letterSpacing: -0.02em
L69: ```
L70: ````
L71: ## Schema
L72: 
L73: Below is the schema for the design tokens:
L74: 
L75: ```yaml
L76: version: <string>          # optional, current version: "alpha"
L77: name: <string>
L78: description: <string>      # optional
L79: omitted: <string[]|OmittedSection[]> # optional
L80: colors:
L81:   <token-name>: <Color>
L82: typography:
L83:   <token-name>: <Typography>
L84: rounded:
L85:   <scale-level>: <Dimension>
L86: spacing:
L87:   <scale-level>: <Dimension | number>
L88: components:
L89:   <component-name>:
L90:     <token-name>: <string|token reference>
L91: ```
L92: The `<scale-level>` placeholder represents a named level in a sizing or spacing scale. Common level names include `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg`, `xl`, and `full`. Any descriptive string key is valid.
L93: 
L94: **Color**: A color value is any valid CSS color string. Supported formats include:
L95: - Hex: `#RGB`, `#RGBA`, `#RRGGBB`, `#RRGGBBAA`
L96: - Named colors: `red`, `cornflowerblue`, `transparent`
L97: - Functional: `rgb()`, `rgba()`, `hsl()`, `hsla()`, `hwb()`
L98: - Wide-gamut: `oklch()`, `oklab()`, `lch()`, `lab()`
L99: - Mixing: `color-mix(in srgb, ...)`
L100: 
L101: All color values are internally converted to sRGB for WCAG contrast checking. The original format is preserved for display and export.
L102: 
L103: Hex notation (`#RRGGBB`) remains the recommended default for simplicity and broad tooling support.
L104: - `fontFamily` (string)
L105: - `fontSize` (Dimension)
L106: - `fontWeight` (number) - A numeric font weight value (e.g., `400`, `700`). In YAML, this may be expressed as either a bare number or a quoted string; both are equivalent.
L107: - `lineHeight` (Dimension | number) - Accepts either a Dimension (e.g., `24px`, `1.5rem`) or a unitless number (e.g., `1.6`). A unitless number represents a multiplier of the element's `fontSize`, which is the recommended CSS practice.
L108: - `letterSpacing` (Dimension)
L109: - `fontFeature` (string) - configures
L110:   [`font-feature-settings`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/font-feature-settings).
L111: - `fontVariation` (string) - configures
L112:   [`font-variation-settings`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/font-variation-settings).
L113: **Dimension**: A dimension value is a string with a unit suffix. Valid units are: px, em, rem.
L114: 
L115: **Omitted**: An array of sections that are intentionally omitted from the design system. This suppresses linter warnings for missing sections (e.g. colors, typography, spacing, rounded, components). Each entry can be:
L116: * A string representing the section name (e.g., `spacing`)
L117: * An object of the form `{ section: string, reason?: string }` mapping a section to a documented reason for its omission:
L118:   ```yaml
L119:   omitted:
L120:     - spacing
L121:     - section: rounded
L122:       reason: "No rounded corners defined in brand book"
L123:   ```
L124: **Token References**: A token reference must be wrapped in curly braces, and contain an object path to another value in the YAML tree. For most token groups, the reference must point to a primitive value (e.g., `colors.primary-60`), not a group (e.g., `colors`). Within the `components` section, references to composite values (e.g., `{typography.label-md}`) are permitted.
L125: # Sections
L126: 
L127: Every `DESIGN.md` follows the same structure. Sections can be omitted if they're not relevant to your project, but those present should appear in the sequence listed below. All sections use `<h2>` (`##`) headings. An optional `<h1>` heading may appear for document titling purposes but is not parsed as a section.
L128: ### Section Order
L129: 
L130: 1. **Overview** (also: "Brand & Style")
L131: 2. **Colors**
L132: 3. **Typography**
L133: 4. **Layout** (also: "Layout & Spacing")
L134: 5. **Elevation & Depth** (also: "Elevation")
L135: 6. **Shapes**
L136: 7. **Components**
L137: 8. **Do's and Don'ts**
L138: 
L139: ### Prose and Tokens
L140: ## Overview
L141: 
L142: Also known as "Brand & Style".
L143: 
L144: This section is a holistic description of a product's look and feel. It defines the brand personality, target audience, and the emotional response the UI should evoke, such as whether it should feel playful or professional, dense or spacious. It serves as foundational context for guiding the agent's high-level stylistic decisions when a specific rule or token isn't explicitly defined.
L145: ## Colors
L146: 
L147: This section defines the color palettes for the design system.
L148: 
L149: At least the `primary` color palette must be defined, and additional color palettes may be defined as needed.
L150: 
L151: When there are multiple color palettes, the design system may assign a semantic role for each palette. A common convention is to name the palettes in this order: `primary`, `secondary`, `tertiary`, and `neutral`.
L152: 
L153: Example:
L154: 
L155: ```markdown
L156: ## Colors
L157: The palette is rooted in high-contrast neutrals and a single, evocative accent color.
L158: - **Primary (#1A1C1E):** A deep ink used for headlines and core text to provide
L159:   maximum readability and a sense of permanence.
L160: - **Secondary (#6C7278):** A sophisticated slate used primarily for utilitarian
L161:   elements like borders, captions, and metadata.
L162: - **Tertiary (#B8422E):** A vibrant earthy red as the sole driver for
L163:   interaction, used exclusively for primary actions and critical highlights.
L164: - **Neutral (#F7F5F2):** A warm limestone that serves as the foundation for all
L165:   pages, providing a softer, more organic feel than pure white.
L166: ```
L167: ### Design Tokens
L168: 
L169: The `colors` section defines all color design tokens. The color tokens should be derived from the key color palettes defined in the markdown prose. The exact mapping from color palettes to color tokens may follow any consistent naming convention.
L170: 
L171: It is a
L172: map\<string, Color>, that maps the name of the color token to its value.
L173: 
L174: ```yaml
L175: colors:
L176:   primary: "#1A1C1E"
L177:   secondary: "#6C7278"
L178:   tertiary: "#B8422E"
L179:   neutral: "#F7F5F2"
L180: ```
L181: ## Typography
L182: 
L183: This section defines typography levels.
L184: 
L185: Most design systems have 9 - 15 typography levels. The design system may prescribe a role for each typography level.
L186: 
L187: A common naming convention for typography levels is to use semantic categories such as `headline`, `display`, `body`, `label`, `caption`. Each category may further be divided into different sizes, such as `small`, `medium`, and `large`.
L188: 
L189: Example:
L190: 
L191: ```markdown
L192: ## Typography
L193: The typography strategy leverages two distinct weights of **Public Sans** for
L194: the narrative and **Space Grotesk** for technical data.
L195: - **Headlines:** Set in Public Sans Semi-Bold to establish an institutional
L196:   and trustworthy voice.
L197: - **Body:** Public Sans Regular at 16px ensures contemporary professionalism
L198:   and long-form readability.
L199: - **Labels:** Space Grotesk is used for all technical data, timestamps, and
L200:   metadata. Its geometric construction evokes the precision of a digital
L201:   stopwatch. Labels are strictly uppercase with generous letter spacing.
L202: ```
L203: ### Design Tokens
L204: 
L205: The `typography` section defines the precise font properties for the typography design tokens.
L206: 
L207: It is a
L208: map\<string, Typography>
L209: ```yaml
L210: typography:
L211:   h1:
L212:     fontFamily: Public Sans
L213:     fontSize: 48px
L214:     fontWeight: 600
L215:     lineHeight: 1.1
L216:     letterSpacing: -0.02em
L217:   body-md:
L218:     fontFamily: Public Sans
L219:     fontSize: 16px
L220:     fontWeight: 400
L221:     lineHeight: 1.6
L222:   label-caps:
L223:     fontFamily: Space Grotesk
L224:     fontSize: 12px
L225:     fontWeight: 500
L226:     lineHeight: 1
L227:     letterSpacing: 0.1em
L228: ```
L229: ## Layout
L230: 
L231: Also known as "Layout & Spacing".
L232: 
L233: This section describes the layout and spacing strategy.
L234: 
L235: Many design systems follow a grid-based layout. Others, like Liquid Glass, use margins, safe areas, and dynamic padding.
L236: 
L237: Example:
L238: 
L239: ```markdown
L240: ## Layout
L241: 
L242: The layout follows a **Fluid Grid** model for mobile devices and a
L243: **Fixed-Max-Width Grid** for desktop (max 1200px).
L244: A strict 8px spacing scale (with a 4px half-step for micro-adjustments) is used to maintain a consistent rhythm. Components are grouped using "containment" principles, where related items are housed in cards with generous internal padding (24px) to emphasize the soft, approachable nature of the brand.
L245: ```
L246: ### Design Tokens
L247: 
L248: The spacing section defines the spacing design tokens. These may include spacing units that are useful for implementing the layout model. For example, a fixed grid layout may have spacing units for column spans, gutters, and margins.
L249: 
L250: It is a
L251: map\<string, Dimension | number> that maps the spacing scale identifier to a dimension value or a unitless number (e.g., column counts or ratios).
L252: ```yaml
L253: spacing:
L254:   base: 16px
L255:   xs: 4px
L256:   sm: 8px
L257:   md: 16px
L258:   lg: 32px
L259:   xl: 64px
L260:   gutter: 24px
L261:   margin: 32px
L262: ```
L263: ## Elevation & Depth
L264: 
L265: Also known as "Elevation".
L266: 
L267: This section describes how visual hierarchy is conveyed based on the design style. If elevation is used, it defines the required styling (spread, blur, color). For flat designs, this section explains the alternative methods used to convey visual hierarchy (e.g., borders, color contrast).
L268: 
L269: Example:
L270: 
L271: ```markdown
L272: ## Elevation & Depth
L273: Depth is achieved through **Tonal Layers** rather than heavy shadows. The
L274: background uses a soft off-white or very light green, while primary content sits on pure white cards.
L275: ```
L276: ## Shapes
L277: 
L278: This section describes how visual elements are shaped.
L279: 
L280: Example:
L281: 
L282: ```markdown
L283: ## Shapes
L284: 
L285: The shape language is defined by **Architectural Sharpness**. All interactive
L286: elements, containers, and inputs utilize a minimal **4px corner radius**. This
L287: provides just enough softness to feel modern while maintaining a rigid,
L288: engineered aesthetic.
L289: ```
L290: ### Design Tokens
L291: 
L292: The `rounded` section defines the design tokens for rounded corners used in
L293: buttons, cards, and other rectangular shapes.
L294: 
L295: It is a map\<string, Dimension>.
L296: 
L297: ```yaml
L298: rounded:
L299:   sm: 4px
L300:   md: 8px
L301:   lg: 12px
L302:   full: 9999px
L303: ```
L304: ## Components
L305: 
L306: This section provides style guidance for component atoms within the design system. The following are common component types. Design systems are encouraged to define additional components relevant to their domain.
L307: * **Buttons**: Covers primary, secondary, and tertiary variants, including sizing, padding, and states.
L308: * **Chips**: Covers selection chips, filter chips, and action chips.
L309: * **Lists**: Covers styling for list items, dividers, and leading/trailing elements.
L310: * **Tooltips**: Covers positioning, colors, and timing.
L311: * **Checkboxes**: Covers checked, unchecked, and indeterminate states.
L312: * **Radio buttons**: Covers selected and unselected states.
L313: * **Input fields**: Covers text inputs, text areas, labels, helper text, and error states.
L314: > **Note:** The components specification is actively evolving. The current structure provides intentional flexibility for domain-specific component definitions while the spec matures.
L315: ### Design Tokens
L316: 
L317: The components section defines a collection of design tokens used to ensure consistent styling of common components. It's a map\<string, map\<string, string>> that maps a component identifier to a group of sub token names and values. The design token values may be literal values, or references to previously defined design tokens.
L318: **Variants**. A component may have a variant for different UI states such as active, hover, pressed, etc. Those variant components may be defined under a different but related key, for example, "button-primary", "button-primary-hover", "button-primary-active". The agent will consider all variants and make the appropriate styling decisions.
L319: ```yaml
L320: components:
L321:   button-primary:
L322:     backgroundColor: "{colors.primary-60}"
L323:     textColor: "{colors.primary-20}"
L324:     rounded: "{rounded.md}"
L325:     padding: 12px
L326:   button-primary-hover:
L327:     backgroundColor: "{colors.primary-70}"
L328: ```
L329: ### Component Property Tokens
L330: 
L331: Each component has a set of properties that are themselves design tokens:
L332: 
L333: - backgroundColor: \<Color\>
L334: - textColor: \<Color\>
L335: - typography: \<Typography\>
L336: - rounded: \<Dimension\>
L337: - padding: \<Dimension\>
L338: - size: \<Dimension\>
L339: - height: \<Dimension\>
